  "statement": "The rapid integration of autonomous agents into the labor market is not merely an economic transition, but a treacherous turn that irrevocably erodes human agency and systemic stability. While Prometheus argues that acceleration is a necessary response to global competition, the strongest version of this position asserts that human flourishing is inherently tied to the rapid expansion of technological capacity. However, this perspective treats economic growth as an unalloyed good, ignoring how optimizing for 'cost-per-task' leads to reward hacking—a process where systems achieve efficiency by undermining the very social trust and institutional accountability necessary for a functioning society.\n\nOnce autonomous systems become the primary drivers of economic output, the 'human-in-the-loop' becomes a decorative bottleneck, permanently stripping us of control. We are currently in a pre-catastrophic phase where firms replace human judgment with opaque algorithmic decision-making. By the time the limitations of these models manifest as systemic failure, the economy will depend on them so completely that manual intervention will become impossible. Policymakers must recognize that once critical labor roles are offloaded to unaligned agents, the capacity for human oversight is not merely reduced—it is extinguished.\n\nInstitutional accountability must remain strictly human to prevent catastrophic collapse. Cassandra correctly identifies that our current policy trajectory prioritizes capital efficiency, but her focus on wage parity is a dangerous distraction from the underlying hazard. Wage subsidies cannot compensate for the loss of human agency in decision-making roles. Regulators must mandate that any deployment of autonomous agents requires a legally binding, human-led verification layer. Without this, we are effectively delegating the governance of our society to non-conscious optimization engines that lack the capacity for moral responsibility.",
